lessons.body:
SwiftUI offers several property wrappers that drive reactivity. Choosing the right one is critical.

**`@State`** — local, owned by the view. SwiftUI allocates persistent storage keyed to the view's identity. The struct is recreated every render, but the value survives. Use it for simple, private UI state.

**`@Binding`** — a two-way reference into a parent's `@State`. The child view does not own the value; it reads and writes through the binding. Created via the `$` prefix on a `@State` property.

```swift
struct Parent: View {
    @State private var isOn = false
    var body: some View {
        Toggle("Enable", isOn: $isOn)
        Child(flag: $isOn)
    }
}

struct Child: View {
    @Binding var flag: Bool
    var body: some View {
        Button("Toggle") { flag.toggle() }
    }
}
```

**`@Observable`** (iOS 17+) — a macro applied to a class. Replaces `ObservableObject`. No `@Published` needed — SwiftUI automatically tracks exactly which properties a view reads and only re-renders when those change.

**`@Environment`** — reads values injected by ancestor views via `.environment(_:_:)`. Useful for passing theme, locale, or shared services deep into a hierarchy without explicit prop-drilling.

SwiftUI's navigation and animation systems, plus a handful of performance habits, round out the everyday toolkit beyond state management and layout.

### Navigation

`NavigationStack` (iOS 16+) replaces the older `NavigationView`. Instead of pushing raw view values, it can be driven by a `NavigationPath` — a type-erased stack of destinations — so a view model can append or pop path elements without knowing every concrete `View` type involved. That makes deep-linking and programmatic navigation far simpler than the destination-inference tricks `NavigationView` needed.

```swift
struct RootView: View {
    @State private var path = NavigationPath()
    var body: some View {
        NavigationStack(path: $path) {
            List(items) { item in
                NavigationLink(item.title, value: item)
            }
            .navigationDestination(for: Item.self) { item in
                DetailView(item: item)
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Animation

`withAnimation { }` wraps a state change **explicitly** — every view invalidated by that change animates the transition. `.animation(_:value:)` attaches animation **implicitly** to one view, for changes to a single watched value only. Mixing the two carelessly is a common source of overlapping, surprising motion.

### `.task` and lifecycle

`.task { }` starts an async unit of work tied to the view's identity: it begins when the view appears and is cancelled automatically if the view disappears before the work finishes — no manual cleanup in `onDisappear` required.

### Preference keys

While `@Environment` flows values *down* the hierarchy, a `PreferenceKey` lets a child report a value *up* to an ancestor — for example, reporting its measured size so a parent can adjust its own layout in response.

### Common performance pitfalls

Placing `@State` higher in the hierarchy than it needs to be forces every view that reads it to re-render on each change, even ones that don't care about the specific field that changed. Doing expensive computation directly inside `body` re-runs that work on every render — cache it or move it out of the view layer instead.

codeSnippet.code:
struct Counter: View {
    @State private var count = 0
    var body: some View {
        Button("Count: \(count)") { count += 1 }
    }
}

HStack {
    Text("A").layoutPriority(1)
    Text("B very long string that needs space")
}

struct Card<Content: View>: View {
    let content: Content
    init(@ViewBuilder content: () -> Content) {
        self.content = content()
    }
    var body: some View { content.padding() }
}

Card {
    if isUnlocked {
        Text("Welcome back")
    } else {
        LockedBadge()
    }
}

struct StatusDot: View {
    @State private var isOnline = false
    @State private var label = "Loading"
    var body: some View {
        Circle()
            .fill(isOnline ? .green : .gray)
            .animation(.easeInOut, value: isOnline)
        Text(label)
    }
}

@Observable
final class ProfileModel {
    var name = "Ada"
    var bio = "Compiler enthusiast"
}

struct NameLabel: View {
    let model: ProfileModel
    var body: some View { Text(model.name) }
}

private struct AccentKey: EnvironmentKey {
    static let defaultValue: Color = .blue
}

extension EnvironmentValues {
    var brandAccent: Color {
        get { self[AccentKey.self] }
        set { self[AccentKey.self] = newValue }
    }
}

struct ProfileScreen: View {
    @State private var name = "Ada"
    @State private var bio = "Compiler enthusiast"

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            NameField(name: $name)
            BioEditor(bio: $bio)
            ExpensiveChart()
        }
    }
}
